<main>

<section class="section">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>01. The architecture of information quality</h2>
    <p>
      Information quality is multidimensional. A source may be authentic
      but outdated, accurate but irrelevant, useful but incomplete, or
      persuasive without being well-supported.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <article class="card">
      <div class="number">01</div>
      <h3>Accuracy</h3>
      <p>Does the information correspond to the underlying facts? Can the specific claim be independently checked?</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <div class="number">02</div>
      <h3>Provenance</h3>
      <p>Where did the information originate? Can you trace its transformations, citations, and ownership?</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <div class="number">03</div>
      <h3>Relevance</h3>
      <p>Does it answer the question you actually need to resolve, or merely discuss a neighboring topic?</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <div class="number">04</div>
      <h3>Timeliness</h3>
      <p>Is the information current for this decision? Different facts have different rates of change.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <div class="number">05</div>
      <h3>Completeness</h3>
      <p>Are important limitations, conditions, exceptions, denominators, and contradictory findings missing?</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <div class="number">06</div>
      <h3>Independence</h3>
      <p>Do multiple sources provide independent evidence, or are they repeating the same original assertion?</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Core distinction:</strong>
    A polished presentation is not evidence. A reputable publisher is
    not infallible. A citation is not automatically relevant support.
    Every important claim must be evaluated in context.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="curriculum">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>02. Advanced intelligence curriculum</h2>
    <p>
      These lessons develop a deeper understanding of how knowledge is
      established, how evidence fails, and how to reason under uncertainty.
      Open each lesson and work through its practical application.
    </p>
  </div>

  <details class="lesson" open>
    <summary>Lesson 1 — Bayesian reasoning: updating beliefs with evidence</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>The central idea</h4>
      <p>
        A rational investigator does not treat every new fact as equally
        important. Evidence should change confidence according to how
        much more likely that evidence would be if a claim were true
        than if it were false.
      </p>
      <div class="equation">
        Posterior odds = Prior odds × Likelihood ratio
      </div>
      <p>
        The prior represents the starting probability. The likelihood
        ratio compares how well the evidence fits competing explanations.
        The posterior represents the updated probability after considering
        the evidence.
      </p>
      <h4>Example</h4>
      <p>
        Suppose a fictional program claims that applicants who complete
        a course are more likely to get a job. A testimonial alone is
        compatible with many explanations: the course helped, the person
        already had strong qualifications, or the job was obtained through
        unrelated experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        Better evidence might compare similar applicants, document the
        employment outcome, explain the sample, and disclose how people
        entered each group.
      </p>
      <h4>Intellectual discipline</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Write down your initial belief before reviewing persuasive evidence.</li>
        <li>Ask what you would expect to observe if the claim were false.</li>
        <li>Distinguish evidence that is merely compatible with a claim from evidence that discriminates between competing explanations.</li>
        <li>Update confidence proportionally rather than switching from certainty to certainty.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Expert insight:</strong>
        Evidence is especially informative when it is much more expected
        under one explanation than under its alternatives.
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson">
    <summary>Lesson 2 — Causal inference: correlation is not causation</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>The central idea</h4>
      <p>
        Two variables can move together without one causing the other.
        Their relationship may arise from a third variable, selection
        effects, reverse causation, or coincidence.
      </p>
      <div class="equation">
        Observed association ≠ established causal effect
      </div>
      <h4>Example</h4>
      <p>
        A company finds that employees who attend optional training
        receive more promotions. This does not prove training caused
        the promotions. Employees who seek training might also have
        more experience, stronger managers, or greater motivation.
      </p>
      <h4>Investigative procedure</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Define the proposed cause and the outcome precisely.</li>
        <li>Establish that the proposed cause occurred before the outcome.</li>
        <li>Identify plausible confounding variables.</li>
        <li>Investigate how participants were selected.</li>
        <li>Examine experimental, quasi-experimental, or longitudinal evidence where appropriate.</li>
        <li>Check whether the conclusion exceeds what the study design can support.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Randomized experiments can help identify causal effects under
        suitable conditions, but poor implementation, attrition,
        noncompliance, and limited generalizability can still undermine
        conclusions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson">
    <summary>Lesson 3 — Provenance and source independence</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>The central idea</h4>
      <p>
        A claim repeated by ten websites may still have only one
        underlying source. Counting repetitions as separate confirmation
        creates false confidence.
      </p>
      <h4>Construct a source lineage</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Identify where the claim first appeared, if possible.</li>
        <li>Follow citations and links backward.</li>
        <li>Check whether sources conducted their own investigation.</li>
        <li>Determine whether apparent corroboration is simply syndication or paraphrasing.</li>
        <li>Look for evidence gathered through a different method or institution.</li>
      </ol>
      <h4>Independence is not binary</h4>
      <p>
        Sources may share datasets, ownership, experts, funding, or
        reporting pipelines. Shared dependencies can make their errors
        correlated. Independent replication is generally more informative
        than numerous derivative summaries.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        Ask: "How many independent paths lead to this conclusion?"
        Do not ask only: "How many pages repeat it?"
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson">
    <summary>Lesson 4 — Statistical literacy and denominator discipline</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>The central idea</h4>
      <p>
        Numbers can be mathematically correct yet misleading because of
        their denominator, comparison group, sampling method, uncertainty,
        or visual presentation.
      </p>
      <h4>Common failures</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Relative versus absolute change:</strong> A 50% increase from 2 cases to 3 cases is one additional case.</li>
        <li><strong>Base-rate neglect:</strong> A seemingly impressive indicator may still produce many false positives when the condition being detected is rare.</li>
        <li><strong>Selection bias:</strong> A sample of enthusiastic customers may not represent all customers.</li>
        <li><strong>Survivorship bias:</strong> Studying successful businesses while ignoring those that failed distorts conclusions about what works.</li>
        <li><strong>Small samples:</strong> A few observations may produce unstable estimates.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing uncertainty:</strong> A point estimate does not reveal how precisely the quantity is known.</li>
      </ul>
      <h4>Questions for every important statistic</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>What is the numerator?</li>
        <li>What is the denominator?</li>
        <li>Who or what was included and excluded?</li>
        <li>What is the comparison group?</li>
        <li>How was the measurement collected?</li>
        <li>What range of values is compatible with the data?</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson">
    <summary>Lesson 5 — Epistemology: what does it mean to know?</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>Separate four epistemic states</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Observed:</strong> Directly recorded or experienced, with measurement limitations acknowledged.</li>
        <li><strong>Inferred:</strong> A conclusion drawn from observations and assumptions.</li>
        <li><strong>Reported:</strong> A statement communicated by another source that has not necessarily been independently checked.</li>
        <li><strong>Unknown:</strong> A question for which the available evidence does not justify a conclusion.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A fifth useful category is <strong>disputed</strong>: multiple
        interpretations or bodies of evidence remain in conflict.
      </p>
      <h4>The ladder of epistemic strength</h4>
      <p>
        Begin with the raw observation. Establish that the measurement
        was valid. Check the provenance. Examine the interpretation.
        Test alternative explanations. Then determine the strength of
        the conclusion.
      </p>
      <p>
        A confident statement is a statement about someone's confidence,
        not necessarily about the quality of their knowledge.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        Intellectual maturity includes being able to say what would
        change your mind and what you cannot currently establish.
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson">
    <summary>Lesson 6 — Cognitive bias and motivated reasoning</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>Why intelligent people can reach poor conclusions</h4>
      <p>
        Intelligence can improve reasoning, but it can also be used to
        defend an existing belief. More elaborate arguments do not
        automatically mean less bias.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Confirmation bias:</strong> Preferentially seeking evidence that supports an existing belief.</li>
        <li><strong>Anchoring:</strong> Giving excessive weight to an initial number, claim, or impression.</li>
        <li><strong>Availability bias:</strong> Overestimating what is memorable or easy to recall.</li>
        <li><strong>Authority bias:</strong> Treating status as a substitute for evidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Motivated reasoning:</strong> Applying different standards depending on the conclusion one wants.</li>
        <li><strong>Overconfidence:</strong> Being more certain than one's evidence and track record justify.</li>
      </ul>
      <h4>Countermeasures</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Write down the strongest alternative explanation.</li>
        <li>Search deliberately for disconfirming evidence.</li>
        <li>Apply the same standard to claims you like and dislike.</li>
        <li>Specify in advance what evidence would change your mind.</li>
        <li>Ask a knowledgeable person to critique the method, not merely the conclusion.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson">
    <summary>Lesson 7 — Information decay and temporal validity</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>The central idea</h4>
      <p>
        Information can become less useful over time even when it was
        originally accurate. Different categories of information have
        different rates of change.
      </p>
      <p>
        Software instructions can become obsolete after a product update.
        Scholarship deadlines change each cycle. Historical facts may
        remain stable, while market prices can change by the hour.
      </p>
      <h4>Build a freshness protocol</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Identify which elements of the claim can change.</li>
        <li>Find the last verified date, not just the page's display date.</li>
        <li>Determine who controls the underlying information.</li>
        <li>Check the current authoritative record.</li>
        <li>Record the date on which you verified it.</li>
        <li>Set a recheck interval appropriate to the rate of change and the stakes.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A publication date alone does not establish currency. An old
        document can describe a current rule correctly, and a newly
        published article can repeat obsolete information.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson">
    <summary>Lesson 8 — Research methods and external validity</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>Different methods answer different questions</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Descriptive research:</strong> What was observed?</li>
        <li><strong>Correlational research:</strong> Which variables were associated?</li>
        <li><strong>Experimental research:</strong> What effect occurred under the studied intervention and conditions?</li>
        <li><strong>Qualitative research:</strong> How do people experience, interpret, or explain a phenomenon?</li>
        <li><strong>Systematic reviews:</strong> What does the eligible body of research collectively indicate, given its methods and limitations?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        No method is universally superior. The appropriate design depends
        on the question, feasibility, ethical constraints, measurement
        quality, and desired inference.
      </p>
      <h4>External validity</h4>
      <p>
        A result observed in one population, setting, time period, or
        implementation may not transfer to another. Examine the conditions
        under which the evidence was produced before applying it elsewhere.
      </p>
      <h4>Publication bias</h4>
      <p>
        Positive or striking results may be more likely to be published
        or publicized. The visible literature can therefore differ from
        the full set of studies that were conducted.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson">
    <summary>Lesson 9 — Signal detection and efficient filtering</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>The central idea</h4>
      <p>
        Filtering systems face two competing errors: accepting bad
        information and rejecting good information.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>False positive:</strong> Treating an unsupported or false claim as reliable.</li>
        <li><strong>False negative:</strong> Rejecting useful or accurate information.</li>
        <li><strong>True positive:</strong> Correctly identifying information that meets the relevant standard.</li>
        <li><strong>True negative:</strong> Correctly identifying information that fails the relevant standard.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The right threshold depends on the cost of each error. A low-stakes
        brainstorming exercise may tolerate more uncertainty than a legal,
        medical, safety, or major financial decision.
      </p>
      <h4>Practical filter</h4>
      <ol>
        <li>Remove material unrelated to the question.</li>
        <li>Screen for source traceability and minimum evidence.</li>
        <li>Prioritize claims that could materially change the decision.</li>
        <li>Investigate high-risk uncertainties first.</li>
        <li>Stop when additional research is unlikely to change the action, unless a mandatory verification standard remains unmet.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson">
    <summary>Lesson 10 — Decision theory and the value of information</summary>
    <div class="lesson-body">
      <h4>The central idea</h4>
      <p>
        Research has a cost. So does acting on poor information. The goal
        is not infinite certainty; it is obtaining enough decision-relevant
        evidence at a reasonable cost while meeting essential safety and
        verification requirements.
      </p>
      <div class="equation">
        Value of research = Expected improvement in decisions − Research cost
      </div>
      <p>
        This is a conceptual decision rule, not a precise formula unless
        the possible outcomes, probabilities, and costs can be estimated.
      </p>
      <h4>Example</h4>
      <p>
        Before applying for an opportunity, checking its official eligibility
        requirements may take five minutes and prevent hours of wasted work.
        Before making a large financial commitment, deeper independent
        verification may be justified because the downside is greater.
      </p>
      <h4>Stopping rule</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Define the decision and minimum evidence required.</li>
        <li>Identify the uncertainties most likely to change the decision.</li>
        <li>Estimate the cost of resolving each uncertainty.</li>
        <li>Research the highest-value uncertainties first.</li>
        <li>Stop when the decision is adequately supported and further research is unlikely to change it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Do not use a favorable cost-benefit calculation to bypass legal,
        ethical, professional, or safety requirements.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<section class="section" id="decompose">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>03. Claim decomposition laboratory</h2>
    <p>
      Complex statements often combine observations, assumptions, causal
      claims, predictions, and recommendations. Separate them before
      deciding whether the argument is sound.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <div class="field">
      <label for="claimText">Enter a statement or argument</label>
      <textarea id="claimText" placeholder="Example: People who complete this certificate get better jobs, so everyone should buy it."></textarea>
    </div>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="factText">Observable or verifiable claims</label>
        <textarea id="factText" placeholder="What could be checked against records or observations?"></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="assumptionText">Hidden assumptions</label>
        <textarea id="assumptionText" placeholder="What must be true for the argument to work?"></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="inferenceText">Interpretation or inference</label>
        <textarea id="inferenceText" placeholder="What conclusion is being drawn from the evidence?"></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="alternativeText">Alternative explanations</label>
        <textarea id="alternativeText" placeholder="What else could explain the observations?"></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="missingText">Missing evidence</label>
        <textarea id="missingText" placeholder="What evidence would be needed to establish the conclusion?"></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="falsifyText">What would weaken or falsify the claim?</label>
        <textarea id="falsifyText" placeholder="Describe a meaningful counterexample or test."></textarea>
      </div>
    </div>

    <button class="btn dark" type="button" id="buildDossier">Build claim dossier</button>
    <button class="btn light" type="button" id="clearDossier">Clear dossier</button>
    <div class="feedback" id="dossierOutput" aria-live="polite"></div>
    <button class="btn light" type="button" id="downloadDossier">Download dossier as text</button>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="comparison">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>04. Conflicting source analysis</h2>
    <p>
      When sources disagree, compare what each actually measured or
      established. Do not resolve a disagreement by automatically
      choosing the most confident, popular, or prestigious source.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <div class="field">
      <label for="question">Question being investigated</label>
      <input id="question" type="text" placeholder="What specific question do these sources address?">
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Dimension</th>
            <th>Source A</th>
            <th>Source B</th>
            <th>Source C</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="sourceRows"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <button class="btn dark" type="button" id="analyzeSources">Analyze comparison</button>
      <button class="btn light" type="button" id="clearSources">Clear comparison</button>
    </div>
    <div class="feedback" id="sourceOutput" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="investigation">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>05. Investigation challenge</h2>
    <p>
      Practice diagnosing weak evidence. Choose the strongest answer
      before revealing the explanation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <span class="tag">Scenario 01 · Causal reasoning</span>
    <h3>A training provider advertises a 90% employment success rate.</h3>
    <p>
      Its website says that 90% of surveyed graduates found work.
      It does not disclose the survey response rate, the time period,
      the number of respondents, whether jobs were related to the training,
      or how employment was verified.
    </p>

    <div id="scenarioOptions">
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario" value="a">
        <span>A. The claim is reliable because 90% is a very high percentage.</span>
      </label>
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario" value="b">
        <span>B. The claim is false because the provider sells training.</span>
      </label>
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario" value="c">
        <span>C. The claim is insufficiently specified; request the denominator, survey methods, timeframe, outcome definition, and independent verification.</span>
      </label>
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario" value="d">
        <span>D. The claim is proven if the provider publishes testimonials from graduates.</span>
      </label>
    </div>

    <button class="btn dark" type="button" id="checkScenario">Check my reasoning</button>
    <div class="feedback" id="scenarioFeedback" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <span class="tag">Scenario 02 · Source independence</span>
    <h3>Five websites repeat the same statistic.</h3>
    <p>
      All five link to the same original press release. None links to
      the underlying study or independently checks the result.
      What is the best conclusion?
    </p>
    <div id="scenario2Options">
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario2" value="a">
        <span>A. Five sources confirm the statistic independently.</span>
      </label>
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario2" value="b">
        <span>B. The repetition establishes popularity, not five independent confirmations.</span>
      </label>
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario2" value="c">
        <span>C. The statistic must be false.</span>
      </label>
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario2" value="d">
        <span>D. Press releases can never contain reliable information.</span>
      </label>
    </div>
    <button class="btn dark" type="button" id="checkScenario2">Check my reasoning</button>
    <div class="feedback" id="scenario2Feedback" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <span class="tag">Scenario 03 · Statistical reasoning</span>
    <h3>A program reports that its success rate doubled.</h3>
    <p>
      The rate increased from 2% to 4%. No sample size, comparison group,
      or uncertainty estimate is provided. What should you investigate?
    </p>
    <div id="scenario3Options">
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario3" value="a">
        <span>A. Only whether the word "doubled" is mathematically correct.</span>
      </label>
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario3" value="b">
        <span>B. The sample sizes, absolute change, measurement method, comparison group, and uncertainty.</span>
      </label>
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario3" value="c">
        <span>C. Whether the report uses attractive charts.</span>
      </label>
      <label class="option">
        <input type="radio" name="scenario3" value="d">
        <span>D. Nothing; doubling always proves a substantial practical improvement.</span>
      </label>
    </div>
    <button class="btn dark" type="button" id="checkScenario3">Check my reasoning</button>
    <div class="feedback" id="scenario3Feedback" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="calibration">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>06. Confidence calibration lab</h2>
    <p>
      Calibration means that your stated confidence corresponds reasonably
      well to how often your judgments prove correct across many cases.
      It is not the same as being persuasive or feeling certain.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Record a prediction</h3>
    <div class="field">
      <label for="prediction">A specific claim that can later be checked</label>
      <input id="prediction" type="text" placeholder="Example: The published application deadline is November 15.">
    </div>
    <div class="field">
      <label for="confidence">How confident are you?</label>
      <select id="confidence">
        <option value="50">50% — uncertain</option>
        <option value="60">60% — slightly more likely than not</option>
        <option value="70">70% — fairly likely</option>
        <option value="80">80% — very likely</option>
        <option value="90">90% — highly confident</option>
        <option value="95">95% — extremely confident</option>
        <option value="99">99% — almost certain</option>
      </select>
    </div>
    <div class="field">
      <label for="evidenceBasis">Why do you hold this confidence?</label>
      <textarea id="evidenceBasis" placeholder="List the evidence, source, and main uncertainty."></textarea>
    </div>
    <button class="btn dark" type="button" id="savePrediction">Record prediction</button>

    <div id="predictionLog" style="margin-top:18px">
      <div class="empty">No predictions recorded yet.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Calibration practice:</strong>
      Later, record whether each prediction was correct. Across many
      predictions, compare your stated confidence with your actual
      accuracy. A single outcome does not determine whether a probability
      judgment was well-calibrated.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="evaluator">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>07. Weighted information evaluator</h2>
    <p>
      Score the available evidence, not your personal preference for the
      conclusion. Unknown criteria should remain zero until investigated.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <form class="panel" id="evaluationForm">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="claim">Claim being evaluated</label>
        <textarea id="claim" required placeholder="Write one specific claim."></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="source">Source or publisher</label>
        <input id="source" type="text" required placeholder="Author, organization, or publication">
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="url">Source URL (optional)</label>
        <input id="url" type="url" placeholder="https://example.com">
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="purpose">Intended use</label>
        <select id="purpose">
          <option>Learning or academic research</option>
          <option>Career or education decision</option>
          <option>Business or financial decision</option>
          <option>General knowledge</option>
          <option>Health, legal, or safety-related decision</option>
        </select>
      </div>

      <div id="ratings"></div>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="redflags">Known concerns</label>
        <textarea id="redflags" placeholder="List missing evidence, contradictory findings, incentives, or uncertainty."></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="nextstep">Next verification action</label>
        <select id="nextstep">
          <option>Find the original source</option>
          <option>Check an official or authoritative record</option>
          <option>Compare independent sources</option>
          <option>Inspect the research methods and data</option>
          <option>Check dates and current requirements</option>
          <option>Consult a qualified professional</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <button type="submit" class="btn dark">Evaluate and save review</button>
      <button type="button" class="btn light" id="resetEvaluator">Clear evaluator</button>
    </form>

    <aside class="score-panel sticky" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="eyebrow">Preliminary result</div>
      <div><span class="score-number" id="score">0</span><span>/100</span></div>
      <div class="meter"><div class="meter-fill" id="meterFill"></div></div>
      <span class="status" id="status">Not assessed</span>
      <h3>Interpretation</h3>
      <p id="interpretation">Rate the criteria to generate a preliminary screening result.</p>
      <h3>Weak or unknown criteria</h3>
      <ul id="issues"><li>Evaluate the evidence and source.</li></ul>
      <h3>Next step</h3>
      <p id="recommendation">Find the original source.</p>
      <button class="btn light" type="button" id="printReview">Print page</button>
      <p class="small">
        Self-assessed scores are not fact-checks or validated probability
        estimates. A high score does not prove a claim true. High-stakes
        decisions require appropriate independent verification.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>08. Verification protocol</h2>
    <p>
      Complete the checks that apply to the claim. Checklist completion
      measures your process, not the truth of the information.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div id="checklist" class="check-grid">
    <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Exact claim defined</strong><small>The statement is specific enough to test.</small></span></label>
    <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Original source located</strong><small>The information has been traced to its origin where possible.</small></span></label>
    <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Date and context checked</strong><small>The information is applicable to this time and setting.</small></span></label>
    <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Evidence inspected</strong><small>The evidence directly supports the claim.</small></span></label>
    <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Independent confirmation sought</strong><small>Sources do not merely repeat one another.</small></span></label>
    <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Alternative explanations considered</strong><small>Competing explanations have been examined.</small></span></label>
    <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Limitations documented</strong><small>Important gaps and uncertainties are recorded.</small></span></label>
    <label class="check-item"><input type="checkbox"><span><strong>Decision threshold met</strong><small>Evidence is appropriate to the consequences of an error.</small></span></label>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong id="checkProgress">0 of 8 checks completed</strong>
    <p id="checkAdvice">Complete the checks that apply before relying on the claim.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>09. Information failure reference</h2>
    <p>Use this reference to match a detected weakness with an appropriate investigative response.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Failure mode</th><th>Diagnostic question</th><th>Corrective action</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Unsupported assertion</td><td>What evidence directly supports the claim?</td><td>Trace citations and locate primary records.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>False precision</td><td>Does the measurement justify this level of detail?</td><td>Inspect methods, sample size, and uncertainty.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cherry-picking</td><td>Were relevant contrary results omitted?</td><td>Search for the full body of relevant evidence.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Selection bias</td><td>Who is absent from the sample?</td><td>Inspect recruitment, inclusion, and exclusion criteria.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Confounding</td><td>Could a third variable explain the relationship?</td><td>Examine the causal model and study design.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Outdated claim</td><td>Could the underlying fact have changed?</td><td>Verify the current authoritative record.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dependent sources</td><td>Do sources share one underlying source?</td><td>Trace provenance and seek independent corroboration.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Misleading framing</td><td>Does the wording exceed what the data show?</td><td>Rewrite the claim in neutral, measurable terms.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unfalsifiable claim</td><td>What possible observation would count against it?</td><td>Clarify testable predictions or limit the conclusion.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>High-stakes uncertainty</td><td>What is the cost of being wrong?</td><td>Require stronger verification and appropriate expertise.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="heading">
    <h2>10. Review history and evidence record</h2>
    <p>
      Keep your findings organized and export them for further work.
      Records are held in page memory and are not automatically retained
      after a reload.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="actions no-print">
    <button class="btn dark" id="exportLog" type="button">Export reviews as CSV</button>
    <button class="btn light" id="clearLog" type="button">Clear review history</button>
  </div>
  <div id="reviewLog" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="empty">No evaluations saved yet.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="dark-panel">
    <div class="eyebrow">Final decision protocol</div>
    <h2>Do not confuse finishing research with achieving certainty.</h2>
    <p>
      A defensible decision states what the evidence supports, what it
      does not support, which uncertainties remain, and why the evidence
      is sufficient—or insufficient—for the specific action.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Define the decision and the claim that matters.</li>
      <li>Identify the strongest available evidence and its limitations.</li>
      <li>Test important alternative explanations.</li>
      <li>Determine the cost of false acceptance and false rejection.</li>
      <li>Meet any mandatory verification or professional requirements.</li>
      <li>Record the conclusion, confidence, and next review trigger.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The goal is not to believe nothing. It is to make your confidence
      proportional to the evidence and your actions proportional to the
      consequences.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

